Make diff -r report a subdirectory that exists in only one tree.
With -r and without -N, skip_dir() doesn't descend into a subdirectory
that the other tree lacks, and sets status 1. But with -r only files are
listed, so nothing named the subdirectory or its files: diff printed
nothing at all and still exited with 1. When the other tree has no entry
of that name, add the skipped subdirectory itself to the listing, as
skip_dir() already does without -r, so that diff prints "Only in DIR:
SUB" as diffutils does. A non-directory of that name in the other tree
is already listed there and reported, so leave that case alone.

---
 editors/diff.c |    8 +++++---
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

--- a/editors/diff.c
+++ b/editors/diff.c
@@ -882,16 +882,18 @@
 		 * Testcase: diff -r /tmp /
 		 * (it would recurse deep into /proc without this code) */
 		struct dlist *const l = state->userData;
-		filename += l->len;
-		if (filename[0]) {
+		const char *name = filename + l->len;
+		if (name[0]) {
 			struct stat osb;
-			char *othername = concat_path_file(G.other_dir, filename);
+			char *othername = concat_path_file(G.other_dir, name);
 			int r = stat(othername, &osb);
 			free(othername);
 			if (r != 0 || !S_ISDIR(osb.st_mode)) {
 				/* other dir doesn't have similarly named
 				 * directory, don't recurse; return 1 upon
 				 * exit, just like diffutils' diff */
+				if (r != 0) /* report it as "Only in" */
+					add_to_dirlist(state, filename, sb);
 				exit_status |= 1;
 				return SKIP;
 			}
